---
// src/pages/parables.astro · 0930 V7
// V7: the numbered timeline is now the index on this page, and the stacked
// BlogCard grid is retired HERE ONLY - BlogCard is untouched and still carries
// the blog feed, the home page and every group view. With forty-two parables
// coming the grid would not hold; the timeline already lists all of them and
// marks the written studies. /parables/timeline is gone (301 to this page, see
// netlify.toml) so there is one index, not two, and its two notes - why John is
// not here, why six passages are not numbered - moved down with it. Its card
// file stays: a crawled card is never removed. The island renders on the
// server on its ALL tab, so all forty-two titles and references are in the
// HTML with no click. Read-throughs still get no row.
// The ON VIDEO rail now lists every parable study with a video: its rule was
// "not already on a card on this page", and nothing here carries a card now.
// V6: the sayings page comes up out of the basement. Its only way in was a note
// near the foot of the timeline, which is itself behind a link — two clicks and
// a scroll from here. It now sits directly under the Foundation Bar, and the
// timeline bar has been moved up beside it from below the grid, so the two
// companion pages read as a pair under the foundation.
//
// No new markup and no new CSS rule. Both are the same <a class="fbar"> block
// with the lower-emphasis modifier that already existed for the timeline bar;
// the modifier was renamed .fbar-timeline -> .fbar-companion, since it now
// carries two, and its margin was retuned for a stacked pair rather than a
// single bar closing the page. The Foundation's own bottom margin came down to
// match, which is what tucks the pair under it. Dark Sayings keeps the full
// title size and stays the loudest thing on the page.
//
// The note and link on the timeline page STAY. That block explains why six
// passages are missing from a list the reader is actively counting, and it
// belongs where the counting happens. Two entry points is correct.
// V5: a bar under the grid opens /parables/timeline. It is the SAME bar as the
// Foundation Bar above — same parchment, same gold spine, same arrow-only hover
// — used a second time rather than a third shape being invented for it. The
// only new CSS is the modifier that gives it its own spacing. Placement is the
// argument: the grid says what is written, and the bar under it says what the
// whole list looks like and how much of it is still to come.
// V4: the ON VIDEO rail now carries only videos whose study is NOT on this page.
// Each study card shows its own video in the card foot, so a rail entry for a
// study in the grid would be the same link a second time. Both parable videos
// belong to studies in the grid, so the rail renders nothing today; it is kept
// for the case it was built for — a video with no study behind it.
// V3: an ON VIDEO rail closes the page — every study in this section that has a
// video, as a row that links out to YouTube. It is DERIVED, not hand-maintained:
// the source is the study's own `watch` frontmatter block (the same block that
// draws the watch card at the top of the study), so a new parable video shows up
// here the moment the study carries one, and no list here has to be edited. No
// new schema was invented for the section. Read-throughs are excluded the way
// the grid excludes them, so a study and its companion never list the same video
// twice; the foundation study is NOT excluded, since its video would belong here
// like any other. Rows link out only — the site never embeds a YouTube iframe.
// V2: Dark Sayings of Old is PROMOTED out of the grid into the Foundation Bar
// above "The Studies" — the one study the rest of the section rests on. The
// study, its file, its route and its association entries are all untouched; it
// is simply no longer one card among many. The old "Going further" heading and
// paragraph are gone, replaced by the bar.
// The Parables — section landing. The front door prose, the Foundation Bar,
// the sayings bar, then the index: every parable in src/data/parables.js, with
// the written studies marked. Publishing a parable study means pointing its
// row's `study` at it there; nothing on this page changes.
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ParableTimeline from '../components/ParableTimeline.jsx';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
// Counts come from the data file, so nothing here is edited when a parable is added.
import { totalCount } from '../data/parables.js';

// THE FOUNDATION — the one study the section rests on, and the target of the
// Foundation Bar. Promoting a different study means changing it here only.
const FOUNDATION_SLUG = 'dark-sayings-of-old';
const foundationHref = `/blog/${FOUNDATION_SLUG}`;

// ON VIDEO — every parable study carrying a `watch` block. The rail used to
// skip studies already shown as a card in the grid; the grid is gone, so none
// are skipped. Read-throughs stay out, so a study and its companion never list
// the same video twice.
const videos = (await getCollection('posts', ({ data }) =>
  data.draft !== true && data.category === 'Parables' && !data.companionOf && data.watch
))
  .sort((a, b) => b.data.date.valueOf() - a.data.date.valueOf());
---

<BaseLayout
  title="The Parables | Nazaryah"
  description="Hebrew symbols, spoken to Hebrew men, out of Hebrew Scripture. Everything added since is invention."
>
  <main class="par">
    <div class="wrap">

      <header class="hero">
        <h1>The Parables</h1>
        <p class="deck">Hebrew symbols, spoken to Hebrew men, out of Hebrew Scripture. Everything added since is invention.</p>
      </header>

      <section class="intro" data-pagefind-body>
        <p>The parables are treated as the shallow end of Scripture. They are handed to children on felt boards and preached to adults as moral encouragement. A man can sit through forty years of sermons on the sower, the talents, and the ten virgins and never once be shown a verse of Torah that any of them was built from.</p>

        <p>The reason is simple, and it is almost never said out loud. Yahushua was a Hebrew teacher speaking to Hebrew men, and every image He used came out of the Scriptures those men had known since childhood. They had no gospels, no epistles, no commentaries, no systematic theology. They had Torah, the Prophets, and the Writings, and nothing else. A lamp, a vineyard, a door, a seed, a shepherd, a debt, a harvest — not one of those was a blank symbol waiting to be filled in. Every one of them already carried a history in the text those men had memorized. <strong>He was not inventing symbols. He was using theirs.</strong></p>

        <p>He said as much Himself, and He said it by quoting the Psalms:</p>

        <blockquote class="par-scripture">
          <p class="par-scripture-text">I will open my mouth in a parable: I will utter dark sayings of old.</p>
          <p class="par-scripture-ref">Psalm 78:2, cited at Matthew 13:35</p>
        </blockquote>

        <p><em>Old</em> is the word that matters. Not new sayings. Old ones, spoken in a form that hid them in the open.</p>

        <p>Cut a parable loose from the Hebrew Scriptures and it has nowhere left to go but a moral. That is how the sower became a lesson about attitude and the ten virgins became a lesson about punctuality. The symbols are still sitting there, still unexplained, and the man in the pulpit fills them himself because nothing else is available to fill them with.</p>

        <p>What follows from that is worse than shallow preaching. Once the meaning has been unmoored, whoever is talking gets to supply it. A man announces that he has uncovered a hidden layer nobody else has seen, assigns his own values to the numbers and the objects, and an audience with no way to test any of it mistakes the confidence for depth. It is not depth. It is one man's opinion wearing a parable, and the parable cannot defend itself because it has already been separated from the only thing that could.</p>

        <p><strong>The test is not complicated. If a meaning cannot be found in the Scriptures the original audience already held in their hands, it was not in the parable. It was added.</strong></p>

        <p>There is a second cost, and it is quieter. Cut loose from the Hebrew Scriptures, the meaning always shrinks to fit what the teacher already believes. A parable about a priesthood becomes advice about being on time. A parable about a covenant becomes a lesson in being pleasant to people. The parable then appears to confirm the doctrine it was just used to illustrate, and the circle closes with nothing outside it left to break the loop.</p>

        <p>These studies add nothing. They put back what was underneath the stories the whole time, and they let the Hebrew Scriptures say what the symbols meant to the men who first heard them.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- THE FOUNDATION BAR — the whole bar is one anchor; nothing nests inside
           it. Full content width, so it sits wider than the intro prose above it
           and squares with the grid below. -->
      <a class="fbar" href={foundationHref}>
        <span class="fbar-eyebrow">The Foundation</span>
        <span class="fbar-row">
          <span class="fbar-title">Dark Sayings of Old</span>
          <span class="fbar-deck">What a mashal is, and why every study below rests on it.</span>
          <span class="fbar-cta">Read first <span class="fbar-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span></span>
        </span>
      </a>

      <!-- THE COMPANION BAR — the same bar as the Foundation above, wearing the
           lower-emphasis modifier, tucked under it. Dark Sayings keeps the full
           title size and reads first. The timeline bar that sat beside it is
           gone: the timeline is on this page now. -->
      <a class="fbar fbar-companion" href="/parables/sayings">
        <span class="fbar-eyebrow">The Sayings</span>
        <span class="fbar-row">
          <span class="fbar-title">Sayings, Not Parables</span>
          <span class="fbar-deck">Six passages counted among the parables that are not parables, and what each one actually says.</span>
          <span class="fbar-cta">Read <span class="fbar-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span></span>
        </span>
      </a>

      <section class="studies" id="timeline" aria-label="The parables in order">
        <div class="studies-head">
          <h2>The Parables in Order</h2>
          <p class="studies-count">{totalCount} parables</p>
        </div>
        <p class="pt-intro">Numbered once, and the number follows a parable into every gospel that tells it. Choose a gospel to read them in that gospel's order, or search by name or reference.</p>
        <div class="pt-index" data-pagefind-ignore>
          <ParableTimeline client:load />
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="pt-note pt-index">
        <h2>Why John is not here</h2>
        <p>
          The gospel of John records no parables. Matthew, Mark, and Luke use the
          word parabole about fifty times between them. John never uses it once.
          What John uses is a different word, paroimia, and only three times — at
          the shepherd and the door in John 10:6, and again in John 16:25 and
          16:29. A paroimia is a figure of speech, a saying set beside the plain
          thing it stands for.
        </p>
        <p>
          The shape of the teaching differs as well. A parable is a story with its
          own people and its own events, and none of them are the speaker. The
          meaning is withheld on purpose. John contains no such stories. The vine,
          the door, the shepherd, the bread, the light — these are single images,
          and Yahushua names Himself as the image in the same breath He speaks it.
          Nothing is withheld.
        </p>
        <p>
          Those images belong to the Hebrew Scriptures that produced them. The
          shepherd of John 10 belongs to Ezekiel 34. The vine of John 15 belongs
          to Isaiah 5 and Psalm 80. Read there, they are not a claim to be the
          Father. Ezekiel's one shepherd is the servant set over the flock by
          Yahuah, and the vine of John 15 has a husbandman standing over it who is
          plainly another.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="pt-note pt-index">
        <h2>Why some familiar passages are not numbered</h2>
        <p>
          Six passages commonly counted among the parables are not on this
          timeline. The lamp under a bushel, the mote and the beam, treasures new
          and old, the blind leading the blind, the guests who cannot repay, and
          the tower builder with the king going to war are sayings rather than
          parables. Each is instruction with an image attached, and each explains
          itself where it stands. They are not set aside.
        </p>
        <p class="pt-notelink">
          <a href="/parables/sayings">Read the sayings <span class="pt-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span></a>
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ON VIDEO — one row per study carrying a `watch` block. Each row is a
           single anchor out to YouTube; nothing nests inside it. -->
      {videos.length > 0 && (
        <section class="vrail" aria-label="Videos in The Parables" data-pagefind-ignore>
          <div class="studies-head">
            <h2>On Video</h2>
            <p class="studies-count">{videos.length} {videos.length === 1 ? 'video' : 'videos'}</p>
          </div>
          <div class="vlist">
            {videos.map((post) => (
              <a class="vrow" href={post.data.watch.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
                <span class="vrow-body">
                  <span class="vrow-title">{post.data.watch.title}</span>
                  {post.data.watch.note && <span class="vrow-note">{post.data.watch.note}</span>}
                </span>
                <span class="vrow-cta">{post.data.watch.cta} <span class="vrow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span></span>
              </a>
            ))}
          </div>
        </section>
      )}

    </div>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* COLOUR NOTE: every colour is declared twice — light first, then again under
     :global(.dark). Site tokens without a dark value would silently win. */
  .par { padding: 3rem 1.5rem 4.5rem; }
  .wrap { max-width: 62rem; margin: 0 auto; }

  .hero { text-align: center; margin-bottom: 3rem; }
  .hero h1 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: clamp(2.2rem, 5vw, 3.1rem); font-weight: 700;
    color: #2b1d11; margin: 0 0 0.9rem;
  }
  :global(.dark) .hero h1 { color: #f0e0bf; }
  .deck {
    font-style: italic; font-size: clamp(1rem, 2.1vw, 1.15rem);
    line-height: 1.6; max-width: 40rem; margin: 0 auto;
    color: #6b5940; text-wrap: pretty;
  }
  :global(.dark) .deck { color: #c9b489; }

  .intro { max-width: 44rem; margin: 0 auto; }
  .intro p {
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: 1.03rem; line-height: 1.78; margin: 0 0 1.4rem;
    color: #2b2317; text-wrap: pretty;
  }
  :global(.dark) .intro p { color: #ded0b4; }
  .intro strong { color: #1e1208; }
  :global(.dark) .intro strong { color: #f5e8d0; }
  .intro a { color: #8b6914; text-decoration: underline; text-underline-offset: 2px; }
  .intro a:hover { color: #4a2808; }
  :global(.dark) .intro a { color: #ba7517; }
  :global(.dark) .intro a:hover { color: #e0b25a; }

  .par-scripture {
    border-left: 3px solid #c4a97d;
    padding: 0.15rem 0 0.15rem 1.15rem;
    margin: 1.9rem 0 1.9rem 0.25rem;
  }
  :global(.dark) .par-scripture { border-left-color: #8a6028; }
  .par-scripture-text {
    font-family: var(--font-family-serif); font-style: italic;
    font-size: 1.12rem !important; line-height: 1.6 !important;
    margin: 0 0 0.45rem !important; color: #3a2c19 !important;
  }
  :global(.dark) .par-scripture-text { color: #f0e0bf !important; }
  .par-scripture-ref {
    font-family: 'Cinzel', Georgia, serif;
    font-size: 10px !important; letter-spacing: 2px; text-transform: uppercase;
    margin: 0 !important; color: #8b6914 !important;
  }
  :global(.dark) .par-scripture-ref { color: #ba7517 !important; }

  /* ── THE FOUNDATION BAR ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
     A bar, not a card: the grid cards carry the scroll-rod curl, the shadow and
     the hover lift from .card in global.css, and this deliberately carries none
     of them. Parchment one step deeper than those cards — the cards sit on
     --color-surface, this sits on --color-surface-elevated, the palette's own
     "slightly darker papyrus". The gold reads on both grounds, so only the
     parchment and the ink are declared twice. */
  .fbar {
    display: block;
    margin: 3rem 0 1rem;
    padding: 1.5rem 2rem;
    background: var(--color-surface-elevated);
    border: 1px solid rgba(139, 111, 62, 0.45);
    border-left: 3px solid #8b6f3e;
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  :global(.dark) .fbar { background: var(--color-dark-surface-elevated); }
  .fbar-eyebrow {
    display: block;
    font-family: 'EB Garamond', Georgia, serif;
    font-variant: small-caps; font-size: 0.72rem; letter-spacing: 0.18em;
    color: #8b6914; margin-bottom: 0.35rem;
  }
  :global(.dark) .fbar-eyebrow { color: #ba7517; }
  /* Title and deck share a row and are centred against each other; the CTA is
     pushed to the far edge. */
  .fbar-row { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 1.5rem; }
  .fbar-title {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    font-family: 'Cormorant Garamond', Georgia, serif;
    font-variant: small-caps; font-size: 1.75rem; line-height: 1.15;
    color: #2b1d11;
  }
  :global(.dark) .fbar-title { color: #f0e0bf; }
  .fbar-deck {
    flex: 1 1 auto; max-width: 60ch;
    font-family: 'EB Garamond', Georgia, serif;
    font-style: italic; font-size: 1rem; line-height: 1.5;
    color: #6b5940;
  }
  :global(.dark) .fbar-deck { color: #c9b489; }
  /* No colour change and no underline on hover — the arrow alone moves. */
  .fbar-cta {
    flex: 0 0 auto; margin-left: auto; white-space: nowrap;
    font-family: 'EB Garamond', Georgia, serif;
    font-variant: small-caps; font-size: 0.85rem; letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    color: #8b6914;
  }
  :global(.dark) .fbar-cta { color: #ba7517; }
  .fbar-arrow { display: inline-block; transition: transform 200ms ease; }
  .fbar:hover .fbar-arrow { transform: translateX(4px); }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .fbar-arrow { transition: none; }
  }

  /* THE COMPANION BARS — the Foundation Bar's lower-emphasis modifier, not a
     second bar. Was .fbar-timeline while the timeline was the only one wearing
     it; renamed, not rewritten, now that the sayings bar wears it too. Still
     the same two declarations and no rule of its own: the title steps down one
     size so the Foundation stays the loudest thing on the page, and the pair
     stacks tight beneath it. Everything else — parchment, spine, gold, hover,
     and the whole mobile block, which targets .fbar — is inherited. */
  .fbar-companion { margin: 0 0 1rem; }
  .fbar-companion .fbar-title { font-size: 1.5rem; }

  .studies { margin-top: 4rem; }
  .studies-head {
    text-align: center; margin-bottom: 1.8rem;
    padding-top: 2.2rem; border-top: 1px solid #d8cfb8;
  }
  :global(.dark) .studies-head { border-top-color: #3a2510; }
  .studies-head h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 1.6rem; font-weight: 700;
    font-variant: small-caps; letter-spacing: 0.03em;
    margin: 0 0 0.35rem; color: #2b1d11;
  }
  :global(.dark) .studies-head h2 { color: #f0e0bf; }
  .studies-count {
    font-family: 'Cinzel', Georgia, serif;
    font-size: 10px; letter-spacing: 2px; text-transform: uppercase;
    margin: 0; color: #8b6914;
  }
  :global(.dark) .studies-count { color: #ba7517; }

  /* ── ON VIDEO ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Rows, not cards, and cut from the Foundation Bar: same parchment one step
     deeper than the grid, same gold spine, same arrow-only hover. It closes the
     page rather than competing with the studies above it, and it wears the
     studies heading so the two sections read as one column. */
  .vrail { margin-top: 4rem; }
  .vlist { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.9rem; }
  .vrow {
    display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 1.5rem;
    padding: 1.15rem 1.75rem;
    background: var(--color-surface-elevated);
    border: 1px solid rgba(139, 111, 62, 0.45);
    border-left: 3px solid #8b6f3e;
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  :global(.dark) .vrow { background: var(--color-dark-surface-elevated); }
  .vrow-body { flex: 1 1 auto; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.3rem; }
  .vrow-title {
    font-family: 'Cormorant Garamond', Georgia, serif;
    font-variant: small-caps; font-size: 1.4rem; line-height: 1.2;
    color: #2b1d11; text-wrap: pretty;
  }
  :global(.dark) .vrow-title { color: #f0e0bf; }
  .vrow-note {
    font-family: 'EB Garamond', Georgia, serif;
    font-style: italic; font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.5;
    color: #6b5940;
  }
  :global(.dark) .vrow-note { color: #c9b489; }
  .vrow-cta {
    flex: 0 0 auto; margin-left: auto; white-space: nowrap;
    font-family: 'EB Garamond', Georgia, serif;
    font-variant: small-caps; font-size: 0.85rem; letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    color: #8b6914;
  }
  :global(.dark) .vrow-cta { color: #ba7517; }
  .vrow-arrow { display: inline-block; transition: transform 200ms ease; }
  .vrow:hover .vrow-arrow { transform: translateX(4px); }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .vrow-arrow { transition: none; }
  }

  /* The card grid drops to one column below Tailwind's sm (640px); the bar
     stacks on the same line so the two never disagree. The gold rule stays. */
  @media (max-width: 639px) {
    .fbar { padding: 1.25rem 1.25rem 1.25rem 1.5rem; }
    .fbar-row { flex-direction: column; align-items: flex-start; gap: 0.5rem; }
    .fbar-deck { max-width: none; }
    .fbar-cta { margin-left: 0; }
    .vrow { padding: 1rem 1rem 1rem 1.25rem; flex-direction: column; align-items: flex-start; gap: 0.55rem; }
    .vrow-cta { margin-left: 0; }
  }

  @media (max-width: 620px) {
    .par { padding: 2.2rem 1.1rem 3.2rem; }
    .hero { margin-bottom: 2.2rem; }
  }
</style>

<style is:global>
  /* COLOUR NOTE: every colour is declared twice — light first, then again under
     .dark. Site tokens without a dark value would silently win. `is:global` is
     required because the timeline is a React island; the `pt-` prefix is what
     keeps these rules from reaching anything else on the site. */
  /* The index sits in a narrower column than the page wrap, so the rows
     read as a list and not a table. */
  .pt-index { max-width: 48rem; margin: 0 auto; }
  .pt-intro {
    font-family: 'EB Garamond', Georgia, serif;
    font-style: italic; font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.6;
    text-align: center; max-width: 40rem; margin: 0 auto 1.8rem;
    color: #6b5940; text-wrap: pretty;
  }
  .dark .pt-intro { color: #c9b489; }

  /* ── GOSPEL TABS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Parchment buttons on the page ground; the chosen gospel fills with the
     same gold that draws every spine on this page. */
  .pt-tabs {
    display: flex;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }
  .pt-tab {
    flex: 1;
    font-family: 'Cinzel', Georgia, serif;
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    padding: 0.6rem 0;
    border: 1px solid rgba(139, 111, 62, 0.45);
    background: var(--color-surface);
    color: #6b5940;
    cursor: pointer;
  }
  .pt-tab:hover { border-color: #8b6f3e; }
  .dark .pt-tab {
    background: var(--color-dark-surface);
    border-color: rgba(186, 117, 23, 0.35);
    color: #c9b489;
  }
  .pt-tab-on,
  .pt-tab-on:hover {
    background: #8b6f3e;
    border-color: #8b6f3e;
    color: #f5e8c8;
  }
  .dark .pt-tab-on,
  .dark .pt-tab-on:hover {
    background: #8a6028;
    border-color: #8a6028;
    color: #f0e0bf;
  }

  /* ── SEARCH ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────*/
  .pt-searchrow {
    display: flex;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.6rem;
  }
  .pt-search {
    flex: 1;
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: 1rem;
    padding: 0.55rem 0.75rem;
    border: 1px solid rgba(139, 111, 62, 0.45);
    background: var(--color-surface);
    color: #2b2317;
  }
  .pt-search::placeholder { color: #9c8560; }
  .pt-search:focus {
    outline: none;
    border-color: #8b6f3e;
  }
  .dark .pt-search {
    background: var(--color-dark-surface);
    border-color: rgba(186, 117, 23, 0.35);
    color: #ded0b4;
  }
  .dark .pt-search::placeholder { color: #8a7857; }
  .dark .pt-search:focus { border-color: #ba7517; }
